  "query_preview": "..\nIssued at 249 PM EST Mon Nov 23 2026\n\nCurrent-Tonight... Overcast conditions persist over the region \ntoday, limiting temperatures to the 60s across much of the area. \nClouds have been able to produce some mist and a few sprinkles at \ntimes, but otherwise, conditions remain dry. There is low confidence \nfor a light shower tonight, but any accumulations will remain \nlargely limited. Low temperatures fall into the upper 50s and low 60s \nacross much of the area. \n\nTuesday-Thursday... A mid-level trough moving through the central \nU.S. deepens into Wednesday as a cutoff low digs over the midwest \nregion. Pulses of short wave energy pass over the Florida peninsula\nthrough the period, ahead of the approaching trough. At the \nsurface, an area of low pressure gradually organizes over the \nsoutheast Gulf into late Tuesday before passing near or south of \nthe local area through Wednesday. Mostly cloudy conditions should \npersist, accompanied by sprinkles or virga through the day on \nTuesday. Attention then shifts to the next heavy rainfall threat \nTuesday night into Wednesday. There is wide spread among \ndeterministic models and ensemble members on how for northward the\napproaching low may wiggle. Acknowledging considerable spread, \nensemble means generally suggest a track near or south of Lake \nOkeechobee. This track would put the greatest emphasis for heavy \nrainfall across the Treasure Coast counties where ensemble means \nsuggest 1-2 inches are possible, and the 90th percentile may point\ntowards localized higher totals of 3-4 inches. The rainfall \nthreat further north and inland is slightly less certain and is \nlargely dependent on how far north the low may move, but cannot \nrule out rainfall totals of an inch or greater northward through \nthe I-4 corridor. Given antecedent conditions and saturated soils,\nhave opted to issue a Flood Watch for the Treasure Coast, \nOkeechobee, and Brevard with the afternoon forecast package, and \nthe Flood Watch is in effect from 1AM Wednesday through 1AM \nThursday. Unlike recent heavy rainfall events, this disturbance \nwill quickly move through the area, and this is not expected to be\na long duration event. \n\nA few lingering showers (20-30%) may persist behind the low and into \nthe Thanksgiving Day forecast. Offshore flow increases Thursday, \nbecoming breezy with wind gusts of 20-30 mph. Seasonal \ntemperatures spreading the mid 70s to near 80 degrees on Tuesday \nwill cool a few degrees each day, ranging the low 70s near and \nnorth of I-4 and upper 70s in vicinity of Lake Okeechobee on \nThanksgiving Day. \n\nThe passing disturbance will result in rapidly building seas and the \nreturn of poor to hazardous beach conditions through the middle of \nthis week. Additionally, high astronomical tides are forecast to \nreturn along the east central Florida coast, enhancing the potential \nfor minor coastal flooding. Rough surf and minor coastal flooding \nwill increase the risk of minor beach erosion, and a high risk of \nrip currents is anticipated along the entire coast. Seas will be \nslow to subside beyond Thursday.\n\nFriday-Sunday (previous)...High pressure builds across the Gulf and \nthe southeastern US into Friday behind the low, resulting in drier \nair and lower rain chances (below 20%) Friday and most of Saturday. \nBy Sunday, some guidance is indicating that a boundary gradually \nlifts northward across the southern Florida peninsula, bringing \nincreasing moisture and a return of some isolated shower chances (20-\n30%) locally, especially across southern portions of the forecast \narea. There remain some discrepancies between model guidance and \nensembles relative to timing and actual evolution of the pattern, so \nrain chances could very well change either upwards or downwards \nthrough this week as the pattern becomes clearer. Below normal \ntemperatures are forecast across east central Florida late week and \ninto the weekend, with the coolest temperatures focused near and \nnorth of the I-4 corridor. While conditions along the coast relative \nto coastal flooding should begin to gradually improve late week and \ninto the weekend, there is still a chance for some minor coastal \nflooding. Beach conditions will also begin to improve as seas \nsubside.",
